((bash-desc-mode . "verbatim") (results ("FAITHFUL-MIN r1" . #hash((recall . #t) (stop . "tool_use") (text . "") (text-chars . 0) (think . "We need answer root cause + one-line fix. The issue: mutable default argument _seen=[] persists across calls, so dedup returns previous items. Fix: def dedup(items, _seen=None): if _seen is None: _seen = [] ... Actually one-line fix? The function has 5 lines. Could change signature to _seen=None and add guard. But user asks one-line fix maybe \"def dedup(items, _seen=None):\" isn't enough; need handle None. Could use `_seen = _seen or []`? Wait if passed empty list? But likely internal. Let's reason.\n\nOriginal:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\n\nRoot cause: default argument `_seen=[]` is evaluated once at function definition; same list object reused across all calls, accumulating prior items.\n\nOne-line fix could be changing signature to `def dedup(items, _seen=None):` and guard? That's two lines. Or one-line fix: `def dedup(items, _seen=set()):`? No still mutable. Use None and inside loop? Could use `_seen = list(_seen) if _seen else []`? Hmm.\n\nMaybe one-line fix: `def dedup(items, _seen=None):` plus `if _seen is None: _seen = []` at start. That's two modified lines? Could combine: `def dedup(items, _seen=None):` and `_seen = _seen or []`? But `_seen or []` fails if _seen is empty list? but _seen usually default None. It returns same list if passed non-empty, new list if None. Could mutate passed list maybe not desired but same as original. The one-line fix might be `def dedup(items, _seen=None):`? Wait the function still would error on first loop `if it not in None`. So need more.\n\nAlternative one-line fix: `_seen = []` inside? The classic is:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    if _seen is None: _seen = []\n    ...\nBut maybe \"one-line fix\" means minimal: change `_seen=[]` to `_seen=None` and add `if _seen is None: _seen = []`. However maybe they want \"one line\" like `_seen = list(_seen) if _seen else []`? Hmm.\n\nLet's think: can fix in one line by replacing default with a callable? e.g. `def dedup(items, _seen=None):` no.\n\nCould use:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    _seen = list(_seen)\n    ...\nBut that creates copy each call, doesn't persist, and original default still created once but not mutated; returns new list with dedup of current items. Wait it changes behavior: it copies initial _seen (empty default) each call, then appends current items. That works, one line inserted after def. But if _seen passed with items, copies them too and returns new list not mutating caller's list. Could be acceptable? But not exactly original intended (which mutated and returned same _seen; caller may rely? The function named dedup usually returns new list maybe). However the original behavior is likely intended to return the same accumulated list? Actually this is a known bug in dedup: they used mutable default to accumulate across calls? The user says \"dedup() sometimes returns items from a previous, unrelated call\" - bug. Fix should make each call independent unless _seen provided? Usually dedup only needs one argument.\n\nOne-line fix: change `def dedup(items, _seen=[]):` to `def dedup(items, _seen=None):`? But then error. So not enough.\n\nWhat exactly is a \"one-line fix\" in code review terms: one line changed/added. The minimal one-line addition after def:\n`if _seen is None: _seen = []`\nAnd signature changed? That's two lines total. Could do one line: `_seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen` plus signature changed. Hmm.\n\nCould we make one-line change without changing signature: \ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\n\nChange to:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    _seen = []\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\n\nThis resets _seen to a fresh list each call; then default argument never used (still created but shadowed). That's a one-line fix (insert `_seen = []` after def). It ignores passed _seen? Actually `_seen` parameter is overwritten; if a caller passed _seen, it would be ignored. Original may have used _seen as accumulator? The problem says unrelated call; likely no one passes _seen. But a library function with optional _seen parameter? Hmm.\n\nBetter one-line: change `def dedup(items, _seen=[]):` to `def dedup(items, _seen=None):` and inside loop? no.\n\nCould change signature to `def dedup(items, _seen=None):` and use `_seen = _seen or []`? That's adding a line. Could do:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    _seen = _seen or []\n...\nNo, `_seen or []` for default empty list returns [] because empty list falsy, so no mutation of default. But if caller passes a populated list, it uses it and mutates it. If caller passes empty list, it replaces with new list (minor difference). And default still created. That is one line inserted. But if original default list remains same? Not mutated. Each call with no _seen uses _seen (default empty, falsy) -> `[]` new list. Good. But the default argument object still created once; not mutated. So no leak. However `_seen or []` is considered less correct than `if _seen is None`. But \"one-line fix\" maybe exactly: `_seen = _seen or []`.\n\nAlternatively:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    return list(dict.fromkeys(_seen + items))? no.\n\nWait the function returns _seen, the accumulated list. If _seen is passed, original appends to and returns same object. The bug is only default. The correct minimal fix is:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    if _seen is None: _seen = []\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\nThis is two changes (signature + guard). User said \"one-line fix\" likely they expect that exact: change `_seen=[]` to `_seen=None` and add `if _seen is None: _seen = []`. But maybe they consider the whole fix can be on one line? Let's phrase: root cause + one-line fix. We can provide both signature and guard. Maybe \"one-line fix\" can be:\n`def dedup(items, _seen=None):` is not enough. Let's test maybe they'd accept `_seen = _seen or []` inserted after def (with original signature). That's truly one line added, and fixes \"returns items from previous call\" because default empty list is no longer mutated. But wait, the default `_seen=[]` still exists as the default value, but the function body shadows it with a fresh list when `_seen` is empty. But is the default list ever mutated? No, because we never call `.append` on it; `_seen = _seen or []` creates new list if _seen is empty; if someone passes `_seen=[]` explicitly? Also new list. If someone passes a non-empty list, we mutate passed list, which is same as original for passed list. Good enough.\n\nBut there is a subtle issue: if caller passes `_seen=[]`, original would append to that new empty list and return it. With `_seen or []`, it creates another list and appends there; caller's list unchanged. That's arguably fine/better.\n\nAlternatively:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    _seen = list(_seen)\n    ...\nThis copies always. If default empty list, returns new list. If caller passes list, doesn't mutate it but returns deduped including prior. But original mutates and returns passed. Could break. The `_seen or []` preserves mutation for non-empty passed list but not for empty passed list. Eh.\n\nThe cleanest is the None guard. The user's \"one-line fix\" may be loose. We can say \"one-line fix\" and show:\n`def dedup(items, _seen=None):` plus guard? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the actual one-line fix using None guard in one line:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    _seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen\n    ...\nBut that's still signature changed + one line. However the whole function could be one line:\n`def dedup(items, _seen=None):` no.\n\nCould use:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    return [it for it in items if it not in _seen]? no because _seen None.\n\nLet's search mentally: This is a common Python gotcha. The recommended fix:\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    if _seen is None:\n        _seen = []\n    ...\nThe \"one-line fix\" likely refers to changing default from `[]` to `None` and adding the `if`. In many code review contexts, they ask \"root cause + one-line fix\" meaning \"mutable default argument; use None sentinel\". The answer should mention both.\n\nBut they specifically wrote the function with `_seen=[]`. Could propose:\n- Root cause: `_seen=[]` is a mutable default argument evaluated once at def time; every call shares the same list object, so `append` persists across calls.\n- One-line fix: `def dedup(items, _seen=None):` and inside... Actually maybe the one-line fix is simply `_seen = list(_seen) if _seen is None else _seen`? Hmm.\n\nLet's craft response:\n\"Root cause: mutable default argument `_seen=[]` is bound once at function definition and shared across calls; appending mutates that shared object, so later calls see previous values. Fix: use `None` sentinel and create a fresh list only when none is passed.\"\n\nOne-line fix (showing function):\n```\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    if _seen is None: _seen = []\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\n```\nBut that's not one line. Could phrase: \"one-line fix (change default + guard):\" But user asked root cause + one-line fix. We can make it one line by:\n`_seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen` plus changing default? still two.\n\nAlternative one-line fix with original signature: add `_seen = _seen or []` after def line:\n```\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    _seen = _seen or []   # fresh list when no explicit list is passed\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\n```\nThis is a single added line. But is `_seen or []` robust? If `_seen` is an empty list passed exp