ems, _seen=None):\n    _seen = _seen or []  # one-line fix\n    ...\n```\nBut `_seen = _seen or []` with `_seen=None` is fine, and simpler. If caller passes empty list, same as above. The original default was `[]`; no one passes empty list expecting mutation. So `_seen = _seen or []` is acceptable and one line. But `if _seen is None` is more precise.\n\nLet's formulate final:\n- Root cause: mutable default argument. The list `[]` is created once when `def` is executed; all calls that don't pass `_seen` share and mutate that same list, so previous call's items appear.\n- Fix: use `None` as default and create the list per call:\n```\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    _seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\n```\nThe guard is the one-line fix (signature change also needed).\n\nAlternatively, if literally only one line may be changed and signature stays:\n```\ndef dedup(items, _seen=[]):\n    _seen = _seen or []\n    ...\n```\nBut I'll mention the preferred fix.\n\nLet's maybe include both to satisfy:\n\"Minimal one-line patch (keeps signature): insert `_seen = _seen or []` as the first line of the body.\"\n\"Cleaner/canonical: use `None` sentinel.\" But user said one-line, so maybe just give the one-line patch and mention better.\n\nHmm, final answer should be concise. Let's craft:\n\n\"Root cause: `_seen=[]` is a mutable default argument; Python binds it once at def time, so every call appends to the same list object, leaking previous results.\"\n\n\"One-line fix (insert as first line of the function):\"\n```python\n_seen = _seen or []   # fresh list unless caller supplied one\n```\nBut wait, if `_seen=[]` default remains, `_seen or []` returns new list because default is empty. That works. But the signature still has mutable default. Is that okay? It fixes the reported bug. Yet a purist would change default to None. The user asked \"Root cause + one-line fix\" not \"bes