cause first call didn't mutate it (it mutated local copy). So `_seen = list(_seen)` is a one-line fix? Wait `_seen = list(_seen)` inside the function body. That one line added before the loop makes it work and keeps signature. But if caller explicitly passes a list, it copies it too, so doesn't mutate caller's list. That might be fine. But is it idiomatic? It still has the mutable default object but never mutated. It's a one-line addition. But the default list is still shared but always copied; harmless.\n\nHowever, the accepted best practice is `None` default. The prompt: \"Root cause + one-line fix.\" Could be from a code review scenario. We should give root cause and the fix.\n\nLet's decide final answer format: short. Include root cause and fixed code. Maybe mention either fix. The one-line fix could be shown as:\n\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    _seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen\n    ...\n\nBut that's two changed lines. To be precise, I'd say: change `_seen=[]` to `_seen=None` and initialize in the body. If you truly want a single-line diff, use `_seen = list(_seen)` at the top? Wait `_seen = list(_seen)` alone with default `_seen=[]`: first call returns new list containing items. But subsequent call? `_seen` default is same original empty list (never mutated). So yes works. But it's confusing because default remains mutable. Better not.\n\nAlternatively:\n\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    return list(dict.fromkeys(items))? That ignores `_seen` param entirely. But the function's `_seen` parameter maybe intended to allow external seen set? The original code returns `_seen`, so if a caller passes a list, it extends and returns it. A pure one-liner replacing entire function:\n\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    return list(dict.fromkeys(items))\n\nBut that drops the `_seen` arg's semantics. However the bug is about \"previous unrelated call\" and the function is named dedup; likely the `_seen` arg was intended as an implementation detail, not public API? But a user reports bug; maybe they call `dedup(items)` only. Still I'd avoid changing behavior.\n\nLet's inspect context? There's no repo specified. The user just pasted code. We need answer, not edit a repo. So answer directly.\n\nThe prompt says \"Root cause + one-line fix.\" Could be they want \"mutable default argument\" and \"def dedup(items, _seen=None): ... _seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen\". I'd phrase:\n\nRoot cause: `_seen=[]` is a mutable default argument, evaluated once when the function is defined and shared across all calls. `dedup` appends to that same list and returns it, so later calls see previous results.\n\nOne-line fix (make the default immutable and create a fresh list per call):\n\ndef dedup(items, _seen=None):\n    _seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen\n    for it in items:\n        if it not in _seen:\n            _seen.append(it)\n    return _seen\n\nBut wait, \"one-line fix\" maybe exactly that line `_seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen`? I can say \"the key one-liner is...\" Hmm.\n\nCould there be a subtle issue with `_seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen` and then the function returns the same list object. If caller passes a list, it mutates and returns it—same as before. If caller doesn't, it creates new list. Good.\n\nBut note: `_seen = [] if _seen is None else _seen` — if `_seen` is passed as an empty list (evaluates false? No we check `is None`, so fine). If passed as None explicitly, it makes new list. Good.\n\nLet's also mention preserving order. Not necessary.\n\nI think answer should be concise. Use final with code block.\n\nBut maybe I should verify Python behavior quickly? Not needed but can. Let's just answer.\n\nHowever, let's think if there is any additional root cause: The function returns the same list that was passed in / default. Even if default were `None` fixed, if the caller does `a = dedup([1]); b = dedup([2], a)` they get accumulated. But the report \"previous, unrelated call\" specifically default arg